>> title: Sicilian Pizza
>> category: Pizzas
>> tags: pizza, tomato, mozzarella, square, italian, oven
>> counters: Pizzeria
>> aka: sicilian, square, square slice, square pie, thick crust, tray pizza, pizza siciliana
>> pairs-with: sicilian-pan-dough, marinara-sauce
>> servings: 12
>> time: 1 hr
>> step.2: layer the cheese right to the edge
>> step.3: ladle the sauce on top in stripes
>> step.4: bake 500°F (260°C) 22 to 28 min
>> step.5: cool 10 min on a rack, then cut in squares

Preheat the #oven{} to 500°F (260°C) with a rack in the lowest slot ~preheat{30%min}.

Layer @low-moisture mozzarella{10%oz}(285 g; grated) over @sicilian pan dough{1%tray}(proved in its 12x16-in pan; from the recipe on this shelf), taking it right out to the metal, where it will catch and fry into the crust the corner squares are fought over for.

Ladle @marinara sauce{1 1/2%cup}(360 mL; from the recipe on this shelf) over @&(~1)cheesed dough{} in three broad stripes rather than a sheet, and scatter @dried oregano{1%tsp} and @grated pecorino{1/4%cup}(25 g) across it.

Bake @&(~1)tray{} on the low rack ~bake{25%min}, until the sauce has gone matte and a spatula under one corner shows a base fried gold and stiff.

Cool @&(~1)pizza{} out of the pan and on a rack ~cool{10%min}, then cut it into twelve squares.

Layer the cheese under the sauce and not over it: that inversion is the whole dish. Cheese laid straight on the dough seals it against the wet sauce, so a crumb this open bakes through instead of steaming, and the sauce on top dries and concentrates rather than melting into the cheese. A Palermo sfincione goes further again — onion, anchovy and breadcrumb over the top, no mozzarella at all — and it is a different recipe rather than a topping change.
